 "rulebook": "Official Baseball Rules (MLB, 2024 edition): 3.01 (the ball), Definitions of Terms: \"catch\" (secure possession in the hand before the ball touches the ground, held long enough to show control), \"in flight\"",
 "deviations": [
  "Definitions (catch): no glove; both hands are Unitree's Dex1-1 two-jaw grippers (sold for the G1, the H2 and the R1, not for the H1-2; mounted on the wrist where the H1-2's palm ends): 90 mm stroke, jaws 80 mm long and 34 mm wide with rubber pads, at most 120 N of grip, opening and closing at most 375 mm/s. A catch is the pads of both jaws of one gripper pressing on the ball from its two sides at the same time (each with a contact whose direction is within 45.0 degrees of the jaws' closing direction: the jaws closed on it, not a ball lying on their tips or edges), at the latest 0.5 s after the ball first touched you; \"held long enough\" is 1.0 s in which the ball stays between the pads of that gripper (breaks of at most 0.02 s) and touches nothing else, without falling. Nothing is welded: the ball is held only by the pads' friction and can slip out.",
  "Definitions (catch): a ball that touches the body (any part other than the grippers and the forearms) before it is caught is not a catch here (the rule would allow trapping it against the body), and neither is a ball held at the end by anything but the jaws of the gripper that caught it (the other hand, a forearm, the body).",
  "The fly ball is not batted: a launcher 40.0 m away sends it at 21.35 m/s, 45.0 degrees above the horizon, 1.0 s after the start, without spin; where it comes down (within 3.0 m of where you stand) is this instance's draw.",
  "Flight: quasi-steady aerodynamics (MuJoCo's ellipsoid fluid model: drag and Magnus lift), still air."
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Catch the fly ball: close the jaws of one of your grippers on the baseball before it touches the ground or your body, and keep it there.",
  "rules": "A baseball (0.073 m across, 0.145 kg, it flies in air with drag) rests on a launcher 40.0 m in front of you, 1.0 m above the ground, and is launched at t = 1.0 s at 21.35 m/s, 45.0 degrees above the horizon, 1.18 degrees to the left of the line from the launcher to you, so that untouched it would come down within 3.0 m of where you stand at the start. Both your hands are two-jaw grippers (90 mm stroke, jaws 80 mm long with rubber pads on their inner faces, at most 120 N of grip) that hold things only by friction; they start open. A catch: the pads of both jaws of one gripper touch the ball at the same time from its two sides, each with a contact whose direction is within 45.0 degrees of the jaws' closing direction (a ball lying on the jaws' tips or edges, or on closed jaws, is not caught), after the ball has passed the top of its arc and at the latest 0.5 s after it first touched you. Before the catch the ball may touch only your grippers and forearms. You succeed when, from the catch, the ball has stayed between the pads of that gripper for 1.0 s (both pads touching it in that way, apart from breaks of at most 0.02 s) touching nothing but that gripper. You fail if the ball touches the ground, or any part of you other than a gripper or a forearm, before the catch; if it is touched before it has passed the top of its arc; if it is not caught within 0.5 s of first touching you; if after the catch it is out of the jaws for longer than 0.02 s (dropped, slipped out or let go: a ball caught again does not count) or touches anything but the gripper that caught it (your other gripper, a forearm, your body, the ground); if you fall (your pelvis lower than 0.5 m above the ground, or any part of you other than your feet on the ground) before the end; or after 9 s.",
  "venue": "You stand on a level field facing the launcher (+x), 40.0 m away. The ball rises to about 11 m and comes down steeply, about three seconds after the launch, somewhere within 3.0 m of you. Your grippers are open, their jaws pointing forwards along your forearms. Still air.",
  "difficulty": "The ball is in the air for three seconds and comes down at about 17 m/s more than a metre away: the body has to walk there at 0.6 m/s and bring a gripper whose open jaws leave 8 mm on each side of the ball onto its line, and the jaws need about 40 ms to close on it, while a ball that meets the gripper at that speed bounces off it in a few milliseconds."
 },
